gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\n+Before it asks for approval or starts a worker, the gate checks that `.ard/<n>/plan.md` exists on `main`. If it does not, the gate posts one comment, adds `ard:needs-human` and records a `gate` event with reason `no-plan`; a check that fails to run is retried on the next tick. Removing a role's request label by hand does not count as that role finishing. With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. In a network round the gate first checks the engineer's `inputs:`: a human-provided path missing from `main` holds it with one \"waiting for inputs\" comment, and when the files land it posts a fresh \"ready for approval\" with a new `round=<n>` marker, even with `require_approval: false`; an earlier approval is stale because the engineer's new `:done` follows it, so a maintainer removes and re-adds `ard:approved`. A `worker` input with a `network: false` worker role, a bad path or an oversized file ends `needs-human`. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. In `ard serve`'s terminal UI the `a` key on a queued issue asks for confirmation, then runs the operator's own `gh issue edit --add-label ard:approved` (or opens the issue URL when `gh` is missing or logged out) and wakes the triage loop; the App never adds `ard:approved`, and under `--dry-run` the call is recorded in the ledger, not run. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved